```apex
public class FamilyService {

    /**
     * Links parents to children in NPSP using the "Parent" relationship role.
     * NPSP's reciprocal trigger (List Setting method) creates the matching
     * Son / Daughter / Child record on the parent side.
     *
     * @param parentIdByChildId  keys = child Contact Ids, values = parent Contact Ids
     */
    public static void linkParents(Map<Id, Id> parentIdByChildId) {
        if (parentIdByChildId == null || parentIdByChildId.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Collect valid pairs and all unique contact Ids
        Set<Id> allContactIds = new Set<Id>();
        List<Map.Entry<Id, Id>> validPairs = new List<Map.Entry<Id, Id>>();

        for (Map.Entry<Id, Id> entry : parentIdByChildId.entrySet()) {
            Id childId = entry.getKey();
            Id parentId = entry.getValue();
            if (childId == null || parentId == null || childId == parentId) {
                continue;
            }
            allContactIds.add(childId);
            allContactIds.add(parentId);
            validPairs.add(entry);
        }

        if (validPairs.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // --- 1 SOQL: fetch any existing relationships involving these contacts ---
        Map<Id, Set<Id>> existingPairs = new Map<Id, Set<Id>>();
        for (Relationship__c rel : [
            SELECT Contact__c, RelatedContact__c
            FROM Relationship__c
            WHERE Contact__c IN :allContactIds
               OR RelatedContact__c IN :allContactIds
        ]) {
            Id a = rel.Contact__c;
            Id b = rel.RelatedContact__c;
            if (a == null || b == null) {
                continue;
            }
            if (!existingPairs.containsKey(a)) {
                existingPairs.put(a, new Set<Id>());
            }
            existingPairs.get(a).add(b);

            if (!existingPairs.containsKey(b)) {
                existingPairs.put(b, new Set<Id>());
            }
            existingPairs.get(b).add(a);
        }

        // Build the list of new relationship records (one per surviving pair)
        List<Relationship__c> newRels = new List<Relationship__c>();
        for (Map.Entry<Id, Id> entry : validPairs) {
            Id childId  = entry.getKey();
            Id parentId = entry.getValue();

            if (hasExistingRelationship(existingPairs, childId, parentId)) {
                continue;
            }

            newRels.add(new Relationship__c(
                Contact__c         = childId,
                RelatedContact__c  = parentId,
                Role__c            = 'Parent',
                Status__c          = 'Current',
                IsPrimary__c       = true
            ));
        }

        // --- 1 DML: bulk insert all new relationship records ---
        if (!newRels.isEmpty()) {
            insert newRels;
        }
    }

    /**
     * Returns true if a Relationship__c already links a and b in either direction.
     */
    private static Boolean hasExistingRelationship(
        Map<Id, Set<Id>> pairs, Id a, Id b
    ) {
        if (pairs.containsKey(a) && pairs.get(a).contains(b)) {
            return true;
        }
        if (pairs.containsKey(b) && pairs.get(b).contains(a)) {
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

**How it meets every requirement**

| Requirement | How it's satisfied |
|---|---|
| One record per direction | We insert the child→parent record (`Role__c = 'Parent'`). NPSP's reciprocal trigger (List Setting) automatically creates the parent→child record with `Role__c` set to *Son*, *Daughter*, or *Child* based on the child's `Gender__c` and the configured lookup. |
| Status = Current | `Status__c = 'Current'` on the inserted record. |
| Idempotent / skip existing | A single SOQL fetches every `Relationship__c` whose `Contact__c` **or** `RelatedContact__c` is in the involved-contact set. An in-memory `Map<Id,Set<Id>>` is keyed in both directions; any pair already linked is skipped. |
| Null / empty input | Early `return` before any SOQL or DML. |
| ≤ 1 SOQL, ≤ 1 DML (200 pairs) | Exactly **one** `SELECT` (the relationship lookup) and **one** `INSERT` (the bulk list). The `IN` clause holds at most 400 Ids, well within the 1 000-value SOQL limit. |
| Bulk-safe | All contacts are collected up-front; the SOQL and DML are each executed once regardless of pair count. |

